==English==

===Pronunciation===
*{{IPA|/ɪˈfrʌnt(ə)ri/}}

===Etymology===
From late 17th century {{F.}} {{term|effronterie||lang=fr}}, from {{term|effronté||shameless, insolent|lang=fr}} < {{OF.}} {{term|esfronté||lang=fro}} < Romance *{{term|exfrontātus}} < {{L.}} {{term|exfrons|exfrōns|barefaced|lang=la}}, from [[prefix]] {{term|ex-||from|lang=la}} + {{term|frons|frōns|forehead|lang=la}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|effronteries}}

# [[insolent]] and [[shameless]] [[audacity]]
#* '''1973,''' John Gerrard, ''The Ascent of Man'', Little, Brown & Company, ISBN 0316109339, page 116,
#*: The most powerful drive in the ascent of man is his pleasure in his own skill. He loves to do what he does well and, having done it well, he loves to do it better. You see it in his science. You see it in the magnificence with which he carves and builds, the loving care, the gaiety, the '''effrontery'''.
#* '''2007,''' Marc Jaffe, ''Best Stories of the American West: Volume One'', Macmillan, ISBN 0765310899, page 302,
#*: It had the '''effrontery''' to put on cosmopolitan airs, even if it had mushroomed out of desert wastes in barely three years.
# an [[act]] of insolent and shameless audacity
#* '''1984,''' Niketas Choniatēs, Harry J. Magoulias, ''O City of Byzantium: Annals of Niketas Choniatēs'', Wayne State University Press, ISBN 0814317642, page 252,
#*: At first, everyone believed that he knew of her improprieties and was simply pretending to be ignorant of them, but from his actions later, when his wife's activities were disclosed, he clearly demonstrated that he was not ignorant of her '''effronteries'''.

